Constructor overloading means defining more than one constructor in a class, all with the class name but differing in the number or types of parameters. The constructor that runs is chosen automatically by the arguments given when the object is created.
Example:
class Point {
    int x, y;
    Point() { x = 0; y = 0; }
    Point(int a, int b) { x = a; y = b; }
}
new Point() calls the first; new Point(3, 4) calls the second.

A default constructor is a constructor that takes no parameters. If a class has no constructor at all, the compiler supplies one, which initializes the data members to their default values (0, false, null). A no-argument constructor written by the programmer is also called a default (non-parameterized) constructor.
Example:
class MyClass {
    private int c;
    MyClass() {
        c = 10;
    }
}

